Chapter: ProbeClosureTarget

 Is the damage chapter-wide, or aimed at the function a partial
 application NAMES?

 `make-adder` and `twin` are character-for-character the same definition
 under two names. `relay-once` is defined, never called, and names
 `make-adder` -- not `twin`. The driver runs `twin` first, then
 `make-adder`.

 `twin 47` then `made 47` means neither is hurt and something else
 explains the earlier result. `twin 47` then a fault means the damage
 lands on the definition a partial application NAMES, and calling it
 chapter-wide contagion is wrong. A fault on `twin` means the damage
 really is indiscriminate.

Section: Shapes

  add3 : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  add3 (x) (y) (z) = x + y + z

 Never named by a partial application.

  twin : Integer -> (Integer, Integer -> Integer)
  twin (n) = add3 n

 Named by relay-once below.

  make-adder : Integer -> (Integer, Integer -> Integer)
  make-adder (n) = add3 n

 Defined, never called. Names make-adder and not twin.

  relay-once : Integer -> (Integer, Integer -> Integer)
  relay-once (n) = make-adder n

Section: Driver

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni ("twin " & show (((twin 5) 20) 22))
    print-line-uni ("made " & show (((make-adder 5) 20) 22))
  end
